e to communities, and doesn't solve congestion (induced demand). So when you say "most places don't have the conditions," the implication is that they should stick with cars. But that status quo already fails: congestion and pollution are worse than ever in most metro areas. The practical choice isn't between perfect transit and easy car use—it's between imperfect transit that improves over time and an unsustainable car system that keeps getting worse. **5. Incremental progress is realistic.** Not every city needs to become Singapore tomorrow. Start with one busy corridor: add a bus lane, improve frequency, coordinate with land use. Show results. Build momentum. There's a huge middle ground between a car-dependent city and a transit paradise. And that middle ground—where buses run decently, trams connect neighborhoods, and trains serve commuters—is achievable almost anywhere with political will. So I'm not claiming every rural town can have a subway. But for the vast majority of urban and suburban areas where congestion and pollution are real problems, public transport can be made effective. The "special conditions" are mostly a matter of priorities.
