oads? That's been tried and proven to fail. Electric cars? They don't solve congestion. Ride-hailing? Often worsens it. So the choice isn't between a perfect transit system and an easy car system. It's between a difficult but proven path and a car-dependent path that guarantees more gridlock and pollution. The evidence from real cities shows that when we commit to public transport, it delivers results. The failures prove only that half-measures don't work—not that the goal is misguided.
